# Does a minimum wage cost jobs?
subtitle: "Why the textbook says a wage floor costs jobs, what Seattle and 138 state rises found, and why the answer depends on how high it goes."
voices: {"a":"gemini:Rasalgethi | an even-handed economist weighing both sides; crisp and fair","take":"lines"}

## Does a minimum wage cost jobs?
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Hours of low-wage work
    y_label: Hourly wage
    demand: {}
    supply: {}
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
      p_label: market
      q_label: hours
    price_floor:
      level: 55
      label: Minimum wage
      show_surplus: true
sources:
    - id: seattle
      title: 'Minimum Wage Increases, Wages, and Low-Wage Employment: Evidence from Seattle'
      authors: Jardim et al.
      year: 2017
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w23532
      finding: Low-wage hours fell about 9%
    - id: states
      title: The Effect of Minimum Wages on Low-Wage Jobs
      authors: Cengiz, Dube, Lindner and Zipperer
      year: 2019
      doi: 10.1093/qje/qjz014
      finding: '138 rises: low-wage jobs barely changed'
    - id: cbo
      title: The Effects on Employment and Family Income of Increasing the Federal Minimum Wage
      authors: Congressional Budget Office
      year: 2019
      url: https://www.cbo.gov/publication/55410
      finding: '$15: 17 million raised, about 1.3 million jobs lost'

    text claim_a "“It always costs jobs”" x 520 y 655 font_size 30 hidden true
    text claim_b "“It never costs jobs”" x 520 y 610 font_size 30 hidden true
    mark strike_a around claim_a kind strike hidden true
    mark strike_b around claim_b kind strike hidden true

    card title "Does a minimum wage cost jobs?"

Does a higher minimum wage help low-paid workers, or cost them their jobs? One side says a higher floor always destroys jobs.
    draw axes claim_a

The other says it never does. Both point to evidence.
    draw claim_b

Start with the textbook. Employers buy fewer hours of work as it gets dearer: demand for labour slopes down.
    draw demand_curve label_D

And more people want work, and longer hours, at a better wage.
    draw supply_curve label_S

Left alone, the market settles on a wage and a number of hours.
    draw guide_lines equilibrium_point label_E label_Pstar label_Qstar

Now a law sets the lowest wage a little above that market wage.
    draw floor_line label_floor

At that wage more hours are offered than employers want to buy. So they buy fewer hours than before: the textbook's lost work.
    draw surplus_guides surplus_arrow label_surplus label_Qd label_Qs

    pause 0.3

Some evidence fits. When Seattle raised its minimum to thirteen dollars in 2016, a University of Washington team found low-wage hours fell about nine percent.
    text cue_sea "Seattle: −9% hours" cites ["seattle"] x 250 y 330 font_size 26

    erase cue_sea

Some doesn't. Across 138 state minimum-wage rises, low-wage jobs barely changed in number: jobs below the new floor vanished, and as many appeared just above it.
    text cue_st "138 states: ≈ 0" cites ["states"] x 250 y 330 font_size 26

The picture can fail where few employers compete for workers: they can pay below the market wage, and then a floor can raise pay without costing jobs.
    point at.ref label_Pstar gesture circle

Where the studies agree is size. Push the floor far above the local wage, and the gap becomes hard to argue away.
    animate price_floor.level 72 duration 3

    erase cue_st

In 2019 the Congressional Budget Office judged that a fifteen-dollar federal minimum would raise pay for 17 million workers, and cost about 1.3 million jobs.
    text cue_cbo "CBO: −1.3M jobs" cites ["cbo"] x 250 y 330 font_size 26

So neither claim survives as stated. The real question is not whether a floor costs jobs, but how high it can go before it does.
    draw strike_a strike_b parallel true

    quiz "A $15 minimum comes to two places. Where is it most likely to cost jobs?"
        right: Where most jobs pay $10: there fifteen dollars is far above the market wage, so the gap between work offered and work wanted is widest.
        * Where most jobs already pay $20
        + Where most jobs pay $10
---
